//! `smoozy-server` — the program the rented machine runs.
//!
//! **This is the whole of the server package's executable material.** What it is,
//! why it is one program rather than three, how a role is chosen and where it
//! finds itself are all at [`smoozy_runtime::the_program`], which is the library
//! side of this file and is where the reasoning lives. This file is the entry
//! point and the three roles' own work.
//!
//! # It is a Linux program and it is gated
//!
//! Every act below is a unix act — a filesystem-named socket, peer credentials
//! read from the kernel, a process started as a child, a link read as a value.
//! **The machine Smoozy rents is never Windows**, so on a system that is not a
//! unix this program refuses in one sentence rather than being absent or answering
//! wrongly. **macOS is a unix and is the desk**, which is what lets the roles be
//! run for real here rather than only reasoned about.
//!
//! **Nothing of the desktop application's is reachable from here**, and nothing
//! here is reachable from it: this file is a binary target and no crate depends on
//! it.
//!
//! # It is a member of its own, and that is what lets it reach the work
//!
//! **This package holds the program; `App/Rented-server runtime/` holds the
//! library, and they are one module answering to one plan** — the same shape
//! `Server protocol and transfers` has carried since step 8a. **Cargo's
//! dependencies belong to a package rather than to a target**, so while this file
//! was a binary target of that library it could reach only what the library
//! reached, and every crate holding machine-side work the supervisor must call
//! — reconciliation's disposition table, the render worker's capture loop, the
//! machine-local queue, the setup run — depends on that library. A library that
//! reached back would close a loop the resolver refuses. **A package nothing
//! depends on can depend on all of them**, which is what this one is. The ruling
//! is at `Audits/Build/The machine program/Decisions record.md`.
//!
//! **What a dependency added here costs.** The machine is the far end of an SSH
//! connection and never opens one. Step 8a's second piece took the SSH client and
//! its C cryptography out of this program's tree, 142 dependency crates down to
//! 19, and that is what keeps the artifact small — its byte length and digest are
//! carried in the signed release manifest, `Server protocol and transfers` B8 and
//! B10 — and what lets this program be checked against its real Linux target at
//! the desk. **Nothing that reaches `smoozy-server-protocol` may be added here.**
//!
//! # What each role does, and what it does not
//!
//! - **The SSH command adapter** is built whole. It reads one framed request on
//!   its standard input, passes it to the supervisor over the machine-local
//!   endpoint, and writes the correlated result on its standard output — which is
//!   `The shape`'s own sentence for the class.
//! - **The server supervisor** performs §3 and §4's own start — the interlock, the
//!   fresh run identifier, the endpoint — and then §4's own dispatch: a framed
//!   request's name routed to the answer that owns it. **Every name the dispatch
//!   routes is answered and every other is refused by name**, and
//!   [`unix::EVERY_REQUEST_THIS_MODULE_KNOWS_ABOUT`] is what says which is which,
//!   with [`unix::WHAT_IS_NOT_WIRED_AND_WHO_OWNS_IT`] naming the owner of each
//!   refusal. **No count is written down here on purpose** — a number in a
//!   sentence beside a list is the fact-in-two-places shape this stage keeps
//!   being bitten by, and the record above is derived from the dispatch itself.
//!   **The rest of a
//!   supervisor's duties belong to other documents and are not built**; what is
//!   missing is stated on the complaints stream at every start rather than left to
//!   be discovered, and [`unix::what_a_supervisor_still_owes`] is the record.
//! - **An operation worker of the render kind is `Rendering`'s render worker**,
//!   from step 10c piece 5's second sitting: [`the_render_worker`] is it, and
//!   the supervisor's line is what starts it. **Every other kind is refused by
//!   kind**, naming the document that owns what the worker of that kind does.
//!   §5.1's table assigns every one of the seven kinds to another document, and
//!   **what program a worker of a kind is and what it is handed is that
//!   document's**. **The supervisor's own side of a start is §14's one unit** —
//!   A4's admission, then the record, the sinks and the start — and what it
//!   invokes is this program's own path with the kind's word after it. **The
//!   seventh kind is different and is started**: W7's role worker has no
//!   operation record, so §5.3's own start is what W8's two acts on the role's
//!   desired state reach, and that is the one worker start every store of which
//!   is this module's own.
//!
//! **Markers used in this file that belong to another document, each with the
//! document it belongs to.** `Server protocol and transfers` — B4, B8, B10, C1,
//! C2, C4, C6, C7, C8, C16, C21.
//!
//! **A marker this plan also defines is never listed here and is written out with
//! its document at the site**, because a header list cannot tell the two apart and
//! a bare one there would resolve to a real and wrong clause.

/// **What a supervisor started by this program does not yet do**, said here and
/// on the complaints stream at every start rather than left to be found.
///
/// **A supervisor that answered health checks while quietly reconciling nothing
/// would be the silent failure this project refuses everywhere else.** So the
/// absence is loud: the sentence goes to the machine's own complaints stream at
/// every start.
///
/// **Nothing in Smoozy reads that stream today, and this says so rather than
/// naming a reader it does not have.** What a reader would read instead is the
/// machine-side event journal — **which is built, and this sentence said it was
/// not until 2026-09-12** — but the standing declaration is not one of the
/// things put into it: `State, identity and recovery` E6a caps one entry, and a
/// sentence this long spent at every start would be an entry refused whole and a
/// reader learning nothing. **So the stream is where it goes and the stream has
/// no reader.** **It is not `Reporting a problem`'s journal tail**: that document's `Reporting a
/// problem` G1 takes the tail over the ordinary connection as a command-path
/// request and its `Reporting a problem` G2 makes it the last entries of a store
/// with a retention floor, registered cursors and a per-entry budget — Smoozy's
/// own event journal, and nothing in that document reads a process's stream at
/// all.
///
/// **Where the stream goes is the machine kind's.** On a container the boot hook
/// this crate composes sends it to the bounded file
/// [`smoozy_runtime::registration_content::THE_FILE_A_SUPERVISORS_OWN_WORDS_GO_TO`] in the
/// package area, which is a stand-in for that journal and says so. **On a systemd
/// machine the unit writes no stream settings**, so where it lands is the service
/// manager's own default and nobody on this project has read what that default
/// is.
///
/// **What removes it** is each owning document's own code. **This paragraph named
/// §3.3's reconciliation and `State, identity and recovery` §4's event journal
/// until 2026-09-12 and both are built**, the loop running at every start and at
/// the clear and every disposition it writes being journalled; what is left of
/// reconciliation is the two halves the fifth narrowing below names. **The
/// request set beyond G12 is each defining document's**, `Server protocol and
/// transfers` §1.2 item 3 being where a package version declares it.
///
/// **NARROWED 2026-09-06, when the machine's own durable operation record
/// landed.** This sentence said the records were not built, and that is no
/// longer true: `State, identity and recovery` §5.1's two records have their
/// form at `smoozy_state::machine_operations` and their store at
/// [`smoozy_runtime::acting::operation_records`], and a supervisor makes and
/// holds that store at every start. **What is still owed is the loop over them
/// and the journal that carries what it writes**, and the sentence now says
/// that instead — narrowing it rather than shortening it, because the thing this
/// constant exists to prevent is a supervisor that looks like it reconciles.
///
/// **NARROWED AGAIN LATER THE SAME DAY, when the event journal landed**, and by
/// exactly one clause: *"it wants the event journal of section 4, which is not
/// built"* is gone, because that store is built, the supervisor makes it at
/// every start, and Z9's refusal is journalled into it.
///
/// **NARROWED A THIRD TIME, 2026-09-07, when §4's dispatch and the worker start
/// were wired**, and this is the largest of the three narrowings. Three sentences
/// stopped being true and are gone: *answers the health request and no other*,
/// *writes none* of the two records, and *nothing here takes a request as an
/// operation*. **Six requests were answered from then** — G12's health answer, the
/// readiness `Server protocol and transfers` §1.1 names, §12's readings, U9's
/// answer to a hold, and P3's two acts on the setup-in-progress state — and a
/// hold answer writes a command record before it acts, which is
/// `Server protocol and transfers` C4.
///
/// **NARROWED A FOURTH TIME, 2026-09-07, when W8's two acts on the role's
/// desired state and W12's worker report were wired.** Two clauses stopped
/// being true and are gone. *"It starts no worker either"* — it starts one, and
/// the sentence now says which and from what. And *"no request this package
/// serves asks for a worker, every one that would being named by a document
/// whose own code this program cannot reach"*, **which was already false when
/// the clause before it was true**: the role worker's start comes from a state
/// this module's own code owns and is behind no wall at all. **The wall clause
/// is kept and narrowed to the six per-operation kinds**, which is what it is
/// true of.
///
/// **The count is no longer written into the sentence.** It said *six* while the
/// dispatch answered six, which is a fact kept in two places and the shape this
/// stage keeps being bitten by; it is now derived from
/// [`unix::EVERY_REQUEST_THIS_MODULE_KNOWS_ABOUT`], whose answered half is
/// itself checked against the dispatch.
///
/// **NARROWED A FIFTH TIME, 2026-09-07, when §6's loop landed**, and this is the
/// clause the whole sentence existed for. *"It runs no reconciliation"* is gone,
/// with the three sentences that followed from it: the loop runs at every
/// supervisor start and at the clear of the setup-in-progress state, every open
/// operation record gets exactly one of the four dispositions, each is written
/// durably before the act it names and journalled, and a worker's report from an
/// earlier run is accepted where *resumed* stands on that operation's record.
///
/// **What is still owed is two halves of it and the sentence now names both.** An
/// outcome that ends a job carries no completeness statement, because
/// `Job files and outputs`' reckoning is in a crate this program may not reach —
/// **an absence recorded as one and never four empty sets**. And nothing here
/// starts the second attempt a *re-run once* writes, because every per-operation
/// kind's own re-run is behind the same wall.
///
/// **NARROWED A SIXTH TIME, 2026-09-19, at step 10c piece 5's second sitting,
/// and both halves above are gone for the render kind.** The wall came down at
/// piece 3. A render's ending written by reconciliation carries `Job files and
/// outputs`' completeness statement, or the stated reason it carries none, on
/// the per-job note; and the line starts the second attempt *re-run once*
/// owes, under the same operation. **The sentence also stopped saying the
/// program starts one worker**: it runs the line and starts a render worker for
/// each job in turn. ~~**What it says instead is the one thing that keeps any
/// render from being launched** — the render-start reading is not taken, the
/// script for it being on record nowhere in this build.~~
///
/// **NARROWED A SEVENTH TIME, 2026-09-23, and the struck sentence above is
/// what came out.** Pieces 13 and 14 wrote the script and took the reading into
/// the driver, and step 10c's fifth pass on Vast watched the reading taken on a
/// real rented machine — `version-ask-1.py`, `reading-1.py` and a recorded
/// reading written beside them. **So this program stopped having a standing
/// reason why no render can be launched**, and went on announcing one at every
/// start for three days. **What ends an attempt now is the attempt's own
/// cause**, written with it.

#[cfg(not(unix))]
fn main() {
    eprintln!(
        "smoozy-server: this is the program a rented machine runs, and every rented machine is \
         a Linux machine. It is built for one target tuple and this is not it."
    );
    std::process::exit(1);
}

#[cfg(unix)]
fn main() {
    let mut argv = std::env::args();
    let invoked_as = argv.next().unwrap_or_default();
    let operands: Vec<String> = argv.collect();
    if let Err(why) = unix::run(&invoked_as, &operands) {
        eprintln!("{why}");
        std::process::exit(1);
    }
}

/// **Where a path that exists on this machine really leads**, every link along
/// it followed — the one reading `Job files and outputs` L3 takes from this
/// machine's own disk (step 10c owed piece 7). Nothing where the
/// path cannot be read.
#[cfg(unix)]
pub(crate) fn where_a_path_leads_on_this_machine(path: &str) -> Option<String> {
    std::fs::canonicalize(path)
        .ok()
        .and_then(|led_to| led_to.to_str().map(str::to_string))
}

/// **`Queue & History` L1's fourth unit, the job output folder, as a job's
/// material names it — and none for a first-frame check** that writes into
/// its range job's folder (`Job files and outputs` P18a): the folder is the
/// range's to keep, empty or not, and the check's own files stay where they
/// are, as every job's rendered files do. **`even_a_checks_folder`** is the
/// erase's, which names the folder only to keep every unit it removes out of
/// it (`Queue & History` X2). The twenty-first notes, note 6.
#[cfg(unix)]
fn the_output_folder_a_jobs_material_names(
    manifest: &smoozy_job_files_machine::manifest::SubmissionManifest,
    record_directory: &str,
    even_a_checks_folder: bool,
) -> Option<std::path::PathBuf> {
    let a_check_writes_into_its_range_jobs_folder =
        smoozy_render_settings_machine::payload::Payload::read_back(&manifest.settings_payload)
            .is_some_and(|payload| payload.the_check_writes_into.is_some());
    if a_check_writes_into_its_range_jobs_folder && !even_a_checks_folder {
        return None;
    }
    crate::the_render_worker::the_job_output_folder_on_this_machine(manifest, record_directory)
        .map(std::path::PathBuf::from)
}


/// **`Job files and outputs` P18a: removing a first-frame check never names the folder it shares
/// with its range job**, while every other job's removal names its own, and
/// the erase still names the check's to keep out of it. The twenty-first
/// notes, note 6.
#[cfg(all(test, unix))]
mod twenty_first_6_a_checks_removal {
    use super::the_output_folder_a_jobs_material_names;
    use smoozy_render_settings_machine::payload::Payload;
    use smoozy_state::identities::{Identity, IdentityKind};

    #[test]
    fn twenty_first_6_removing_the_check_leaves_the_range_jobs_folder() {
        let job = Identity::issue(IdentityKind::Job).expect("randomness");
        let mut manifest = smoozy_rendering_machine::composition::testing::a_manifest(&job, "5.2", "Scene");
        let its_own = the_output_folder_a_jobs_material_names(&manifest, "/nowhere", false);
        assert!(its_own.is_some(), "an ordinary job's removal names its folder");

        let mut payload = Payload::read_back(&manifest.settings_payload).expect("a payload");
        payload.the_check_writes_into = Some(Identity::issue(IdentityKind::Job).expect("randomness").as_text());
        manifest.settings_payload = payload.as_carried();
        assert_eq!(the_output_folder_a_jobs_material_names(&manifest, "/nowhere", false), None);
        assert_eq!(the_output_folder_a_jobs_material_names(&manifest, "/nowhere", true), its_own);
    }
}

/// **A root or a package area's location this machine reads, judged by where
/// it really leads here** as well as by its text (`Job files and outputs` L3
/// and `Job files and outputs` N5; step 10c owed piece 7). Every place this program takes
/// the recorded root goes through this, so a root written through a link into
/// an area wiped at every boot is refused before any job is given a folder
/// under it.
#[cfg(unix)]
pub(crate) fn a_root_on_this_machine(
    written: &str,
) -> Result<
    smoozy_job_files_machine::layout::MachineRoot,
    smoozy_job_files_machine::layout::WhyThatIsNotARoot,
> {
    smoozy_job_files_machine::layout::MachineRoot::chosen_where_it_really_leads(
        written,
        where_a_path_leads_on_this_machine,
    )
}

/// **Why a recorded root was refused, in the words the setup's own failure
/// shows behind *What the server said*** (`Server Setup` S2a; Rule 9: never
/// a refusal's name as the language renders it). Added 2026-10-16, step 10c's
/// owed piece 6: a setup run on a root that leads into a wiped area stopped
/// with `ItLeadsUnderASharedTemporaryArea` as its reason.
#[cfg(unix)]
pub(crate) fn a_refused_root_in_words(
    written: &str,
    why: &smoozy_job_files_machine::layout::WhyThatIsNotARoot,
) -> String {
    use smoozy_job_files_machine::layout::WhyThatIsNotARoot as Why;
    match why {
        Why::ItIsNotAnAbsolutePath => {
            format!("Smoozy's folder, {written}, is not a full path.")
        }
        Why::ItIsUnderASharedTemporaryArea => {
            format!("Smoozy's folder, {written}, cannot be under /tmp or /var/tmp.")
        }
        Why::ItHasARelativeSegment => {
            format!("Smoozy's folder, {written}, contains a . or .. part.")
        }
        Why::ItLeadsUnderASharedTemporaryArea => {
            format!("Smoozy's folder, {written}, cannot link into /tmp or /var/tmp.")
        }
        Why::WhereItLeadsCouldNotBeRead => {
            format!("Could not resolve Smoozy's folder, {written}.")
        }
        Why::ItDoesNotFitTheBudget(_) => {
            format!("Smoozy's folder path, {written}, is too long.")
        }
    }
}

